#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097783} :activity/id #uuid "00c1cd54-a7af-5240-860b-4432978fec0c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097807 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the chain rule in the following form:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = f'(g(a)) \\cdot g'(a)\n$$\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that\n\n- $g$ is differentiable at $a$,\n\n- $f$ is differentiable at $g(a)$.\n\nThen $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $a$, and we compute its derivative as follows.\n\nWe start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is differentiable at $a$, it is also continuous there. Hence $g(x) \\to g(a)$ as $x \\to a$. For $x$ sufficiently close to $a$, we have $g(x) \\ne g(a)$, so we may write\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a} \n= \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{g(x) - g(a)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $x \\to a$, and using the product rule for limits (which applies since both limits exist), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘g)^{′}(a) & ={lim}_{x\\to a}(\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} \\\\  & ={f}^{′}(g(a))\\cdot {g}^{′}(a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "d34fc877-5572-53a2-b957-2c5d44a488f3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13330 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "9ad2def5-511c-587f-9847-fa7af8e06405" :step/math-academy-id 466057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097872 :tutorial/content "The **chain rule** for differentiation states that the derivative of a composite function $f(g(x))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nThere is some intuition behind this rule: if we think of the derivatives as fractions, the ${\\textrm{d}g}$'s would appear to cancel out.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see how the chain rule can be used to differentiate a function like \n\n$$\nf(x) = (1+2x)^3,\n$$\n\nfirst, notice that the function can be written as a composite function\n\n$$\nf(g) = g^3, \\qquad g(x) = 1+2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}g}({g}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+2x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6{g}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(x) = 1+2x$ for $g,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6(1+2x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll provide a derivation of the chain rule at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "743ad997-8732-5c0f-9676-ff1d44f3b513" :tutorial/math-academy-id 415 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a2d483f7-5abb-5070-9d8d-2db12d3f0358" :step/math-academy-id 773 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097784}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097784 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096400 :question/id #uuid "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-181" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^4+x^3)^5,$ calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g}  = 5g^4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3})^{4}(12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674252} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186620984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674246}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "7f46eb68-796d-5c0d-a89b-b1dbdf45d975" :step/math-academy-id 774 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097785}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096401 :question/id #uuid "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-182" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{1-x^2},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nHere, we've used $u$ instead of $g$ for the intermediate variable. \n\nLet $u=1-x^2.$ Then $y = \\sqrt u = u^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}  = -2x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657684} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186597617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657682}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "60f88dd0-c1bd-546f-9dac-f440401fd02b" :step/math-academy-id 777 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097786}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097786 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097873 :tutorial/content "The chain rule can also be expressed using primes instead of Leibniz notation.\n\nThe chain rule, in prime notation, states that\n\n$$\n(f(g(x)))' = f'(g(x)) \\cdot g'(x).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58c36c82-b739-55f9-8e99-4df1e0cbde24" :tutorial/math-academy-id 418 :tutorial/title "Another Notation for the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "90637184-1911-5cfb-bf2c-dbdad69086a3" :step/math-academy-id 780 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097787}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097787 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096402 :question/id #uuid "2c37ae73-233d-5e0d-87ff-05cacc6d078b" :question/math-academy-id "e-183" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-3x}},$ calculate $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x)) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{g}} = g^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x)^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2(1-3x)^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493336} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572194}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"} :step/id #uuid "35489675-a0a1-59ea-b1f7-ab794d855f54" :step/math-academy-id 781 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097750}}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096888}]}
